  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Look at cost per mile, not just the payment</h2>
    <p>A car's monthly payment is only part of what it costs. Total cost of ownership adds fuel or electricity, insurance, maintenance, registration, financing, and the biggest line most people skip: depreciation, the value the car loses while you own it. The 2 calculators in this section compare options on that full basis.</p>

    <h3>Electric vs. gas</h3>
    <p>EVs usually cost less per mile to run, but they often cost more to buy and insure. The <a href="../../calculators/ev-vs-gas.html">EV vs. gas calculator</a> weighs those against each other. Where you charge decides much of the answer: home charging at residential rates is typically far cheaper than DC fast charging, which can approach or exceed the per-mile cost of gas. In the calculator's default example the EV saves about $892 a year to run, but its higher price leaves the gas car $611 ahead over 8 years. Higher annual mileage, cheap home electricity, or expensive gas tip it the other way. Note that the federal EV tax credit ended for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025.</p>

    <h3>Lease vs. buy</h3>
    <p>A lease payment is set mostly by the drop from the negotiated price to the residual value, plus a finance charge based on the money factor. Multiply the money factor by 2,400 to compare it with a loan's APR: a money factor of 0.0025 is equivalent to about 6%. The <a href="../../calculators/lease-vs-buy-car.html">lease vs. buy calculator</a> compares the 2 over the lease term and over the years you would actually keep the car. Leasing tends to cost more over the long run, since you are always paying for a car's steepest years of depreciation, but it can make sense if you want a new car every few years or drive few miles.</p>

    <h2>Negotiate the price either way</h2>
    <p>Whether you lease or buy, the negotiated price drives the cost. On a lease, a lower price directly lowers the depreciation you pay for. Ask for the price, residual, money factor, and every fee in writing, and compare offers using those numbers rather than the monthly payment, which is easy to lower by stretching the term or asking for more money down.</p>
  </div>
</section>
